// Text to integer and integer to text for i8, i16, i32, i64, isize, u8, u16, u32, u64 and
// usize (the last four are 64 bits wide): 17609 generated texts, each with the error kind and
// byte offset it must report, or the value it must give and the text that value prints as.
//
// Generated by a table generator with a fixed seed; do not edit by hand. The rule: `[+]digits`
// (`[+-]digits` for a signed type); empty text is Empty at 0; a lone sign is InvalidChar at 0;
// no digit where the first belongs is InvalidChar there ("-" is one for an unsigned type);
// bytes after the digits are Trailing at the first of them, even when the digits are out of
// range; digits out of range with nothing after them are Overflow at the digit that first
// takes the value above the limit (documented on int_overflow). The texts: the minimum,
// maximum and their neighbours, powers of ten and two and their neighbours, random values,
// each with a plus sign, runs of leading zeros up to 30, a trailing byte, an inserted space,
// underscore or comma, and leading space; plus malformed signs and non-ASCII digits. An
// accepted value is printed by `as string`, by append_to and by an f-string hole, and the
// three must equal its decimal text.
// Cases from Rust library/coretests/tests/num/mod.rs (test_int_from_str_overflow and the
// from_str error tests) and library/coretests/tests/num/{i8,i16,i32,i64,u8,u16,u32,u64}.rs

import std::io

// The reason `t.try_parse<T>()` raises: kind None at 0 when it accepts `t`.
fn <T impl FromStr> reason_of(const t: string) -> ParseError {
    try {
        var v = t.try_parse<T>()
        return ParseError { at: 0, kind: ParseErrorKind::None }
    } catch e: ParseError {
        return e
    }
}

